.TH "MAW-PACKAGES" "7" "" "maw 0.2.3" "maw manual"
.SH NAME
maw-packages \- installing, removing, finding, updating, adopting
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Packages you want on the machine are declared in \fBmaw.nix\fR, one list per source:
.PP
.EX
packages = {
  xbps = [ "foot" "niri" ];
  flatpak = [ "com.slack.Slack" "org.gnome.Calculator" ];
  cargo = [ "bat" "typos-cli@1.24" "https://github.com/user/tool.git" ];
  go = [ "github.com/jesseduffield/lazygit" ];
  uv = [ "ruff" "httpie@3.2.3" ];
  npm = [ "prettier" ];
};
.EE
.PP
maw writes those lists; you change them with the commands below. \fBmaw activate\fR installs anything declared but missing, before it links files. Nothing is ever removed just because it isn't declared.
.SH "INSTALLING"
.PP
.EX
maw install foot                                   # from the void repos
maw install hello-cli                              # not in xbps: asks before using crates.io
maw install cargo:bat@0.24                         # a crate, pinned to a version
maw install cargo:https://github.com/user/tool.git # a crate from git
maw install go:github.com/jesseduffield/lazygit    # a go program
maw install com.slack.Slack                        # a flatpak app, by its app id
maw install uv:ruff                                # a python program from pypi
maw install uv:git+https://github.com/user/tool    # a python program from git
maw install npm:prettier                           # a javascript program from npm
maw install foot --dry-run
.EE
.PP
.EX
install foot
record foot in maw.nix
create modules/foot.nix
.EE
.PP
A bare name goes to whichever source already declares it, a flatpak app id (\fBcom.slack.Slack\fR) to Flathub, and anything else to xbps. If xbps doesn't have it, maw looks for that exact name on Flathub, crates.io, PyPI, and npm, and asks first; with more than one match, it asks which:
.PP
.EX
prettier isn't in xbps; install one of:
  1  npm:prettier 3.6.2  Prettier is an opinionated code formatter
  2  cargo:prettier 0.1.0  ...
which? [1]
.EE
.PP
Without a terminal it stops and names the prefixes to use instead. When no source has the name, maw looks in the AUR and nixpkgs and offers to \fBmaw help source packages\fR from one; \fBmaw install aur:<name>\fR or \fBnixpkgs:<name>\fR goes straight there. The draft opens in your editor, and \fBmaw install <name>\fR then builds and installs it. Library crates and npm packages with no commands are refused before any question, since there's nothing to install; add them to a project with \fBcargo add\fR or \fBnpm install\fR instead. A \fBflatpak:\fR, \fBcargo:\fR, \fBgo:\fR, \fBuv:\fR, \fBnpm:\fR, or \fBxbps:\fR prefix skips all of that. Python and npm packages always need their prefix. \fB@version\fR pins a crate, go program, python program, or npm package to that version; without it you get the latest.
.PP
Each source needs its tool: \fBcargo\fR, \fBgo\fR, \fBuv\fR, \fBflatpak\fR, or \fBnpm\fR (Void's \fBnodejs\fR). When one is missing, maw stops and says what to install: \fBerror: uv isn't installed; maw install uv first\fR. \fBmaw install uv uv:ruff\fR does both, xbps first.
.PP
Then maw installs (\fBsudo xbps-install\fR, \fBsudo flatpak install\fR, \fBcargo install --locked\fR, \fBgo install\fR, \fBuv tool install\fR, or \fBnpm install -g\fR), records the package in \fBmaw.nix\fR, and, when the registry knows the program and the repo has no module or \fBstatic/\fR dir for it yet, creates its module the way \fBmaw new\fR does, importing any config already on disk. Then it activates. A package that's already installed is only recorded. A name nothing has is an error.
.PP
cargo installs into \fB~/.cargo/bin\fR, go into \fB$GOBIN\fR (or \fB$GOPATH/bin\fR, or \fB~/go/bin\fR), and uv and npm into \fB~/.local/bin\fR (npm with \fB~/.local\fR as its global prefix, so nothing needs root). maw leaves your shell config alone, but warns after an install if that dir isn't on your \fBPATH\fR, naming the line to add.
.SS "Python programs"
.PP
uv gives each python program its own environment. A program that needs an older Python, or an extra package in its environment, gets them in \fBconfig.nix\fR, by its name:
.PP
.EX
maw.uv.openconnect-sso = {
  python = "3.12";                     # uv downloads it if the system has another
  requirements = [ "setuptools<81" ];  # installed alongside, like uv tool install --with
};
.EE
.PP
They apply whenever maw installs or upgrades it: \fBmaw install uv:openconnect-sso\fR, and \fBmaw sync\fR.
.SS "Flatpak apps"
.PP
Flatpaks come from Flathub (maw adds the remote if it's missing) and are installed system-wide, for every user. Each declared app also gets a wrapper in \fB~/.local/bin\fR named after the last part of its id, so configs and shells can run it by that name: \fBcalculator\fR runs \fBflatpak run org.gnome.Calculator\fR. To pick another name, in \fBconfig.nix\fR:
.PP
.EX
maw.flatpakNames = { "us.zoom.Zoom" = "zoom-meet"; };
.EE
.PP
App launchers like fuzzel find flatpaks through their desktop entries either way. \fB@<commit>\fR pins an app to an exact build, which is how generations record them. An app's permissions and environment are declared with \fBlib.flatpak\fR in a module; see \fBmaw help modules\fR.
.SH "REMOVING"
.PP
.EX
maw remove foot
maw remove bat          # a crate, found by name
maw remove lazygit      # a go program, by its binary or path
maw remove calculator   # a flatpak, by its id or wrapper name
.EE
.PP
Removes the package and drops it from \fBmaw.nix\fR. xbps also removes dependencies nothing else needs, and flatpak runtimes nothing uses any more; go programs are deleted from the bin dir. Its module stays, so reinstalling brings the config back; delete \fBmodules/foot.nix\fR yourself if you're done with it. \fB--dry-run\fR prints the plan.
.SH "LOOKING THINGS UP"
.PP
.EX
maw query           # packages you installed or declared, in every source
maw query bat       # one of them
maw search term     # every source; [*] installed, [~] draftable
maw search npm:term # one source: xbps, flatpak, cargo, go, uv, npm, aur, or nixpkgs
maw info bat        # details, plus whether maw manages it
.EE
.PP
\fBquery\fR lists what you installed by hand plus everything declared, flagging the two kinds of mismatch: \fB(undeclared)\fR for installed but not in \fBmaw.nix\fR, \fB(missing)\fR for declared but not installed. Crates and go programs are marked with their source. \fBsearch\fR looks in xbps, Flathub, crates.io, PyPI (exact names only, since PyPI has no search), and npm, and prints names the way \fBmaw install\fR takes them. AUR and nixpkgs matches come last, marked \fB~]\fR: they can't be installed as they are, only drafted into a template. A source whose tool isn't installed is skipped; one that doesn't answer gets a warning. nixpkgs is searched through [search.nixos.org (https://search.nixos.org). \fBinfo\fR shows the version, whether it's installed and declared, the module or \fBstatic/\fR dir holding its config, and where the registry puts that config.
.SH "UPDATING"
.PP
.EX
maw sync
.EE
.PP
Upgrades the system (\fBxbps-install -Su\fR), then every flatpak, crate, go, python, and npm program that isn't pinned to a version. Then it moves each \fBmaw help source packages\fR template that follows releases to its upstream's newest, and rebuilds every source package whose template is ahead of what's installed. That's how maw updates itself: its template follows maw's releases. Moved templates are repo changes, so sync then activates and records them as a generation. A source package that fails to build is a warning, and the rest of sync goes on; sync exits with an error at the end, naming what didn't build. Pinned packages stay put until you install a different version, and so do xbps packages a rollback (\fBmaw help rolling back\fR) held back; \fBmaw sync --release\fR releases those first.
.PP
A template follows releases when it downloads a tagged release from GitHub, GitLab, Codeberg, or sourcehut with \fB${version}\fR in the url, like \fBdistfiles="${homepage}/archive/refs/tags/v${version}.tar.gz"\fR. maw reads the repo's tags (\fBgit ls-remote\fR), takes the newest plain version matching the url's tag (\fBv${version}\fR finds \fBv0.3.0\fR, skipping pre-releases like \fBv0.3.0-rc1\fR), and rewrites \fBversion\fR, \fBrevision\fR, and \fBchecksum\fR. \fBmaw src update <name>\fR does the same for one template. A \fB# maw: hold\fR line keeps a template where it is.
.SH "ADOPTING"
.PP
.EX
maw adopt
maw adopt --dry-run    # print the checklist instead
.EE
.PP
Lists everything \fBstatus\fR calls \fBundeclared\fR in your editor, one line each, every line starting as \fBkeep\fR:
.PP
.EX
# maw adopt: `keep` records it in maw.nix, `here` for this machine only, `skip` (or deleting the line) ignores it from now on

# packages (xbps)
keep firefox
skip base-devel

# packages (cargo)
keep https://github.com/user/tool.git

# services (system)
keep NetworkManager
skip agetty-tty3
.EE
.PP
Save and quit, and \fBkeep\fR lines are recorded in \fBmaw.nix\fR like \fBmaw install\fR or \fBmaw sv enable\fR would, while \fBskip\fR lines go under \fBignored\fR:
.PP
.EX
ignored = {
  packages = {
    xbps = [ "base-devel" ];
  };
  services = {
    system = [ "agetty-tty3" ];
  };
};
.EE
.PP
Ignored things stay installed and enabled; maw just stops mentioning them. Nothing is installed, removed, or restarted by \fBadopt\fR. Only packages you installed by hand are offered, never their dependencies.
.PP
